1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
The scanner reads characters and emits tokens. The parser only ever sees tokens.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Longest match: at every position take the longest legal token, then start again.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Twelve tokens: one keyword, three identifiers, two numbers, one relational operator, five punctuation marks.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Every scanner decision fits in a finite automaton, so the scanner cannot remember context.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
FIRST(A) is the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from A, plus epsilon if A can vanish.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Three passes settle this grammar: FIRST(S) = FIRST(A) = FIRST(B) = {a, b, epsilon}.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately after A, and the start symbol always gets the end marker.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
FOLLOW(A) is {a, b} and FOLLOW(B) is {a, b, dollar}: the only difference is the end marker.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A production goes into M[A, a] for every a in FIRST of its right side, and into FOLLOW(A) if the right side can vanish.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
M[S, a] holds two productions, because epsilon is legal exactly when a can follow S - and a can.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
On the GATE 2010 grammar every cell holds exactly one production, so that grammar is LL(1).

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The parser keeps a stack and matches the top against the lookahead; a match pops, an expansion pushes the right side reversed.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A shift pushes the next token onto the stack. A reduce replaces a right-hand side on top of the stack by its left side.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A handle is a right-hand side sitting on top of the stack at the moment it is correct to replace it.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
At every valid state the stack holds a viable prefix: a prefix of a right-sentential form that stops at the handle.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Seven reduces and eight shifts accept aaadbbb, and each reduce is one internal node of the parse tree.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
An item is a production with a dot showing how much of its right side the parser has already seen.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Closure: if the dot sits before a nonterminal, add every production of that nonterminal with the dot at the left.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Goto moves every dot one symbol right and closes again. The collection here has seven states.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
On the GATE 2026 grammar the LR(0) table has five shift-reduce conflicts, and SLR(1) on the same automaton has none.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One grammar, one LR(0) automaton of eleven states, and three ways of deciding when a state may reduce.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
SLR reduces A to d on every terminal in FOLLOW(A), and c is in FOLLOW(A) - but only because of a different production.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
LALR keeps an exact lookahead per item and merges only states with the same core, so the conflict disappears.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
LALR always has exactly as many states as LR(0): here eleven, and on S to CC seven against ten for canonical LR(1).

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
T goes to T star U: the nonterminal recurs on the left of its own operator, so star groups to the left.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
P goes to Q plus P: the recursion is on the right, so plus groups to the right.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Precedence comes from depth: star binds tighter because S reaches T and T reaches U, one level below.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The dangling else is a shift-reduce conflict that every language resolves the same way: shift.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A syntax-directed definition attaches attributes to grammar symbols and a rule to each production.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
On aab the actions fire in reduce order - 2, then 3, then 1 - so the scheme prints 231.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The same tree emits three-address code: one temporary per operator, deepest subtree first.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
GATE 2022: 20 hash 10 percent 5 hash 8 percent 2 percent 2 evaluates to 80.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
GATE 2010: S to a S a, b S or c has no LL(1) conflict and no LR(1) conflict, so it is both.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
GATE 2007: the if-then-else grammar is not LL(1) because it is ambiguous - the page counts two parse trees.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
GATE 2025 Set 2: SLR reduces in state four on c, LALR does not, so the grammar is LALR(1) but not SLR(1).

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
GATE 2026 Set 2: the LR(0) ACTION table for that grammar has five shift-reduce conflicts.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: augment the grammar. Step two: compute FIRST and FOLLOW to a fixed point and stop when a pass changes nothing.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step three: build the LL(1) table and count the productions inside each cell, not the cells.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Steps four and five: build the LR(0) automaton once, then add lookaheads one rung at a time.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Four slips: a missing end marker, associativity read off the operator, LALR assumed to have more states, and cells counted instead of entries.

